        body: "Protecting stained glass is a specialty detail. The engineer specifies an exterior protective glazing system — laminated impact-rated glass in a framed system mounted outside the historic windows — engineered for the wind-borne debris pressures at that wall height and zone, with a ventilated airspace between the protective glass and the stained glass so trapped heat and moisture do not damage the historic panels. The mounting frames anchor into the surrounding masonry or framing with the full calculated loads, and the system is detailed for maintenance access so the stained glass can still be serviced. This approach satisfies the building code's opening-protection requirements while leaving the interior view of the stained glass untouched.\n\nThe rest of the envelope follows the standard hardening path with church-specific attention. Entry doors — often large, decorative, and numerous — are replaced with pressure- and impact-rated assemblies or fitted with listed shutters, since doors are breached more often than windows. Fellowship hall storefront and classroom windows get impact replacements. The roof replacement is specified as a complete wind assembly: the covering's uplift rating, the underlayment, the sheathing attachment, and the edge metal all designed for the calculated pressures, because the 2020s taught the Gulf Coast that edge flashing is where roofs start to fail. The engineer reviews every product submittal against the pressure schedule — substitutions are where retrofits quietly lose their rating.",
