        question: "Why do warehouse roofs fail in hurricanes?",
        answer: "Uplift at the edges and corners, usually starting with the flashing. Wind accelerating over a big flat roof creates extreme suction in the corner and edge zones — far higher than the field of the roof — and once the edge flashing or coping peels, the wind gets under the membrane or panels and unzips the assembly. The engineering defense is a tested roof assembly with zone-rated fastening heaviest at the edges and corners, plus edge metal designed for the same pressures. Most warehouse roof failures trace to substituted clips, widened fastener spacing, or under-designed edge flashing.",
