        body: "Buoyancy is checked vessel by vessel. The engineer calculates the uplift on each tank at empty, partial, and full conditions against the design flood elevation, and sizes anchors, saddles, and pad foundations so no credible fill level lets a tank float. Piping connections get flexible elements where a tank might shift, and the engineer verifies that the building slab itself resists hydrostatic uplift — a slab that heaves takes every tank pad with it. Vents terminate above the flood elevation with goosenecks so water cannot enter the product.\n\nWastewater pretreatment and CO2 safety are engineered for the flood case. Screens, equalization, pH adjustment, and chemical feed are elevated or built watertight, and the engineer confirms the system can be shut down and restarted without discharging off-spec waste to the city. CO2 detection heads, alarms, and ventilation controls are mounted above the waterline, and the emergency ventilation sequence is designed to clear low-lying CO2 even when the lower level has been compromised by water — because the gas hazard outlasts the flood.",
