        body: "Redundancy is quantified, not gestured at. The engineer sizes the chiller plant, air handlers, and pumps so the building holds collection setpoints with the largest single component out of service, and the piping and ductwork are valved and dampered so any unit can be isolated without shutting down its zone. The automation system sequences the standby equipment automatically — a collection cannot wait for someone to drive in during a storm — and the engineer tests the failure scenarios in the sequence of operations before the design is issued.\n\nFlood and storm protection wrap the mechanical design. Equipment sits above the design flood elevation, below-grade storage gets waterproofing plus sump pumps on emergency power, and outdoor air intakes close automatically in storm mode via interlock with the building's weather response. The engineer also designs the restart sequence: after the storm, the systems come back in an order that re-establishes humidity control before temperature, because a gallery that cools down while still humid condenses moisture onto the collection.",
