        heading: "Wind, Debris Impact, and the Tested Envelope",
        body: "The envelope design treats pressure and impact as simultaneous loads. The engineer sizes reinforced concrete or masonry walls and the concrete roof deck for the shelter design wind speed, then verifies the assembly against the debris impact criteria — the wall must stop the missile without breaching and without spalling lethal fragments off the interior face. Every penetration is part of the tested system: ventilation louvers, conduit entries, and the door are specified as assemblies rated for the same impact, because the envelope is only as strong as its weakest opening.\n\nConnections carry the impact into the structure. The engineer details wall-to-foundation, wall-to-roof, and door-frame-to-wall connections for the combined pressure and impact reactions, with the anchorage designed for the cyclic, reversing nature of wind loading rather than a single static push. The foundation itself is sized for the overturning and uplift the shelter wind speeds generate — a safe room that slides or overturns is a failed design regardless of its walls — and the engineer verifies the soil capacity under the rapid loading the storm applies.",
