        body: "Electrical design balances the marine environment against maintainability. The engineer circuits the pier lighting in segments so failures stay local, specifies marine-grade fixtures with replaceable components, and designs the grounding grid for the seawater environment with cathodic considerations where steel piles are involved. The shore-end service — meter, main disconnect, and distribution — sits above the design flood elevation in a hardened enclosure, and the single-operation storm disconnect is labeled and accessible to the pier staff who will actually throw it.\n\nWater and sewage are engineered for the pier's reality. The potable line gets backflow protection at the shore, insulation or heat trace where needed, and drain-down valves at the low points; the sewage ejector station is floodproof with redundant pumps, high-level alarming to the shore, and a force main routed with the same wave-tolerant supports as the other utilities. The storm procedure — de-energize, drain, secure — and the recommissioning sequence — inspect, test, flush, verify — are delivered as part of the design documents so the pier reopens on engineering, not optimism.",
